1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,783
So far your boarding party has read its way through rooms.

2
00:00:05,683 --> 00:00:07,422
Today it walks instead.

3
00:00:09,222 --> 00:00:14,874
This is a yard, with a caretaker, a sentry and a locked door,

4
00:00:15,324 --> 00:00:20,541
and every bit of it is in four files you can read.

5
00:00:21,441 --> 00:00:24,050
You'll make it with one command,

6
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:26,239
play it through once,

7
00:00:26,689 --> 00:00:28,863
and then take it apart.

8
00:00:30,063 --> 00:00:34,846
This half of the class gets a mission of its own.

9
00:00:35,746 --> 00:00:37,485
Same command as before,

10
00:00:37,935 --> 00:00:41,848
with a different template after the dash t: away.

11
00:00:43,648 --> 00:00:45,822
Press Enter at the question,

12
00:00:46,272 --> 00:00:49,750
and it tells you the mission is ready.

13
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:52,824
Then the fetch line, once,

14
00:00:53,274 --> 00:00:56,317
to bring the libraries up to date.

15
00:00:57,217 --> 00:01:00,261
Both lines matter more than usual here.

16
00:01:00,711 --> 00:01:04,189
This mission has no pictures of its own.

17
00:01:04,639 --> 00:01:09,422
Its map is drawn with two art packs that missions share,

18
00:01:09,872 --> 00:01:14,654
and these two commands are what fetch them and unpack them.

19
00:01:16,454 --> 00:01:19,933
Open the folder, trust it, and run lint,

20
00:01:20,383 --> 00:01:22,557
and it comes back clean.

21
00:01:23,757 --> 00:01:25,930
A server and two consoles.

22
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:31,163
The ship starts beside the relay, so nobody has to fly.

23
00:01:32,063 --> 00:01:34,237
Handheld, Boarding Party, beam down.

24
00:01:36,037 --> 00:01:37,776
And there's the difference:

25
00:01:38,226 --> 00:01:40,835
a map, and you on it.

26
00:01:41,735 --> 00:01:43,909
Click the ground to walk.

27
00:01:44,359 --> 00:01:48,272
Click a person, and you walk up and talk.

28
00:01:49,172 --> 00:01:53,520
This is a room, exactly like the ones you've written:

29
00:01:53,970 --> 00:01:58,317
buttons, and one of them only a medic is offered.

30
00:02:00,117 --> 00:02:02,291
He mentions a medical kit,

31
00:02:02,741 --> 00:02:07,959
and now it's on the map, where a moment ago it wasn't.

32
00:02:08,859 --> 00:02:11,467
The sentry is the hard part.

33
00:02:11,917 --> 00:02:14,961
From the weapons console I open Fire,

34
00:02:15,411 --> 00:02:18,020
arm it, and click the sentry.

35
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:21,093
It drops a power cell.

36
00:02:21,543 --> 00:02:23,717
The keycard opens the door,

37
00:02:24,167 --> 00:02:26,776
the cell goes into the beacon,

38
00:02:27,226 --> 00:02:28,965
and that's the game.

39
00:02:30,165 --> 00:02:32,339
Now look at the folder.

40
00:02:33,239 --> 00:02:35,413
Nine files came with it,

41
00:02:35,863 --> 00:02:38,907
and four of them are the mission.

42
00:02:40,707 --> 00:02:45,054
Two are in a folder called ground: the map itself,

43
00:02:45,504 --> 00:02:50,287
and a short list of the kinds of ground there are.

44
00:02:51,187 --> 00:02:52,926
Then the fact sheet,

45
00:02:53,376 --> 00:02:55,115
which holds everything else.

46
00:02:56,015 --> 00:02:57,754
And the story file,

47
00:02:58,204 --> 00:03:00,813
where one line loads the ground

48
00:03:01,263 --> 00:03:03,872
and one card opens the party.

49
00:03:04,772 --> 00:03:08,685
There's no Python here, and you won't write any.

50
00:03:09,885 --> 00:03:13,363
First, the yard as the crew sees it,

51
00:03:13,813 --> 00:03:17,726
on the Science console, the moment she beams down.

52
00:03:18,626 --> 00:03:21,235
The landing pad she stands on,

53
00:03:21,685 --> 00:03:22,989
the caretaker's tent,

54
00:03:23,439 --> 00:03:26,917
and the relay house at the east end.

55
00:03:28,717 --> 00:03:34,370
Now click the map file in VS Code, and you get a picture.

56
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:37,428
Next lecture is about that picture.

57
00:03:38,328 --> 00:03:41,372
Today press Text, and read what's underneath.

58
00:03:43,172 --> 00:03:44,911
It has three parts.

59
00:03:45,811 --> 00:03:49,289
The header says what this area is called,

60
00:03:49,739 --> 00:03:52,783
and where someone who beams down stands.

61
00:03:53,683 --> 00:03:56,726
The legend says what each character means:

62
00:03:57,176 --> 00:03:58,915
a dot is dust,

63
00:03:59,365 --> 00:04:01,539
a capital W is wall.

64
00:04:02,439 --> 00:04:06,787
And some lines carry an at sign and a name.

65
00:04:07,237 --> 00:04:08,976
That's called a mark.

66
00:04:09,426 --> 00:04:12,035
It gives a cell a name,

67
00:04:12,485 --> 00:04:15,528
so a record can say, stand here.

68
00:04:17,328 --> 00:04:20,372
Under three dashes is the map itself.

69
00:04:20,822 --> 00:04:22,996
You can find the pad,

70
00:04:23,446 --> 00:04:25,185
the tent beside it,

71
00:04:25,635 --> 00:04:28,678
and the relay house with its door.

72
00:04:29,578 --> 00:04:31,317
Notice what isn't here.

73
00:04:31,767 --> 00:04:34,376
No caretaker, no sentry, no key.

74
00:04:34,826 --> 00:04:37,000
Only the places they stand.

75
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:39,939
Back in the game,

76
00:04:40,389 --> 00:04:44,737
the keycard lies in the dust where the sentry walks,

77
00:04:45,187 --> 00:04:48,230
and Old Marrow sits beside his tent.

78
00:04:49,130 --> 00:04:53,043
Both of them are records in the fact sheet.

79
00:04:54,843 --> 00:04:57,452
The fact sheet has seven sections,

80
00:04:57,902 --> 00:05:00,946
and four of them you've written before.

81
00:05:01,846 --> 00:05:04,020
Three of them are new:

82
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:06,209
props, people and hostiles.

83
00:05:08,009 --> 00:05:10,183
Here's one of the props.

84
00:05:10,633 --> 00:05:14,546
A heading, a fence, and a line of description.

85
00:05:15,446 --> 00:05:18,489
Two fields put it on the map.

86
00:05:18,939 --> 00:05:21,113
Area names the map file.

87
00:05:21,563 --> 00:05:25,041
Mark names a mark in that file's legend.

88
00:05:25,941 --> 00:05:28,985
And one field says what it does.

89
00:05:29,435 --> 00:05:32,478
Item means you can pick it up.

90
00:05:34,278 --> 00:05:36,887
A person is the same shape.

91
00:05:37,337 --> 00:05:41,250
And this line is a join you already know.

92
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:44,309
It's the key of a room.

93
00:05:45,209 --> 00:05:50,426
So a room on the ground is the room you've always written,

94
00:05:50,876 --> 00:05:53,920
with one difference: nobody walks into it.

95
00:05:54,370 --> 00:05:59,152
It belongs to the thing or the person that opens it.

96
00:06:00,352 --> 00:06:04,265
In the story file, one line loads the ground.

97
00:06:04,715 --> 00:06:06,454
You never change it.

98
00:06:06,904 --> 00:06:09,948
A new map is found by itself.

99
00:06:11,748 --> 00:06:15,661
Now remember the Boarding Party app on the handheld,

100
00:06:16,111 --> 00:06:18,720
with Kesh Relay waiting in it.

101
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:24,837
That comes from a card at the end of the story file.

102
00:06:25,737 --> 00:06:29,215
Put it beside the one from Lecture 2.

103
00:06:29,665 --> 00:06:31,839
The first room is gone,

104
00:06:32,289 --> 00:06:35,333
because a map has no first room.

105
00:06:35,783 --> 00:06:37,957
And there's a new word:

106
00:06:38,407 --> 00:06:41,015
the area to beam down into.

107
00:06:41,915 --> 00:06:44,089
Three words here are yours.

108
00:06:44,539 --> 00:06:48,887
The signal, the name on the tile, and the area.

109
00:06:50,687 --> 00:06:52,426
Last comes the ending.

110
00:06:53,326 --> 00:06:56,804
This is the last screen of the win,

111
00:06:57,254 --> 00:07:02,037
and the sentence at the left is in the fact sheet.

112
00:07:02,937 --> 00:07:04,676
This quest carries it,

113
00:07:05,126 --> 00:07:09,039
with a second sentence beside it for a loss.

114
00:07:09,939 --> 00:07:15,591
And an answer at the beacon either completes that quest, or fails it.

115
00:07:16,041 --> 00:07:17,780
That's the whole ending.

116
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:23,763
The long example of all this is a mission called Dawnline.

117
00:07:24,663 --> 00:07:27,272
It doesn't come with the game,

118
00:07:27,722 --> 00:07:29,896
and you don't need it.

119
00:07:31,696 --> 00:07:33,435
Look at the numbers.

120
00:07:33,885 --> 00:07:36,493
Six maps where you have one.

121
00:07:36,943 --> 00:07:39,552
Forty-two props where you have six.

122
00:07:40,452 --> 00:07:45,235
But every row is the same kind of thing as yours.

123
00:07:47,035 --> 00:07:50,513
One thing its maps have that yours doesn't:

124
00:07:50,963 --> 00:07:54,441
a way from one map to the next.

125
00:07:54,891 --> 00:07:57,935
You'll build that in two lectures' time.

126
00:07:58,835 --> 00:08:01,443
For now, change three small things,

127
00:08:01,893 --> 00:08:04,937
play each one, and put them back.

128
00:08:06,737 --> 00:08:10,650
Next time you draw a map of your own.
